{"id":"8d8982fb-8ba4-4d33-8955-cab2680ac400","object":"chat.completion","created":1785036212,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    tokens = case['tokens']\\n    calls = case['calls']\\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (c['end'], c['start']))\\n    n = len(sorted_calls)\\n    prev = [-1] * n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        for j in range(i - 1, -1, -1):\\n            if sorted_calls[j]['end'] <= sorted_calls[i]['start']:\\n                prev[i] = j\\n                break\\n    dp = [0] * n\\n    for i in range(n):\\n        score = sorted_calls[i]['score']\\n        choose = score + (dp[prev[i]] if prev[i] >= 0 else 0)\\n        not_choose = dp[i - 1] if i > 0 else 0\\n        dp[i] = max(choose, not_choose)\\n    selected_indices = set()\\n    i = n - 1\\n    while i >= 0:\\n        if i == 0:\\n            if dp[0] > 0:\\n                selected_indices.add(0)\\n            break\\n        score = sorted_calls[i]['score']\\n        choose = score + (dp[prev[i]] if prev[i] >= 0 else 0)\\n        not_choose = dp[i - 1]\\n        if choose >= not_choose:\\n            selected_indices.add(i)\\n            i = prev[i]\\n        else:\\n            i -= 1\\n    selected_call_ids = [sorted_calls[i]['call_id'] for i in selected_indices]\\n    selected_calls = [sorted_calls[i] for i in selected_indices]\\n    selected_calls.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\\n    result_tokens = []\\n    cursor = 0\\n    for call in selected_calls:\\n        start = call['start']\\n        end = call['end']\\n        result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:start])\\n        result_tokens.extend(call['replacement_tokens'])\\n        cursor = end\\n    result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:])\\n    return {\\\"selected_call_ids\\\": selected_call_ids, \\\"tokens\\\": result_tokens}\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task AGENT-TF-02. The input case has tokens array and calls array. Each call has call_id, start, end, score, replacement_tokens. The goal is to select a subset of calls that do not overlap in token positions and maximize some metric? The output should have selected_call_ids (list of call_ids) and tokens (list of strings). The tokens output likely is the original tokens with selected calls applied? But the output schema says tokens is an array of strings. The instruction doesn't specify the algorithm. Look at the cases: they all have same tokens and calls. The expected output for these cases is not given. Possibly the task is to greedily select non-overlapping calls with highest score? Or maybe it's about the union of intervals? Since the cases are identical except nonce, the output should be same for all. Need to infer the algorithm.\n\nGiven the name \"AGENT-TF-02\", might be about token filtering/selection. In many previous tasks, we need to pick calls that don't overlap and replace tokens with replacement tokens. The tokens output likely is the resulting token sequence after applying the selected calls. The selected_call_ids are the ids of those selected.\n\nWe need to determine selection criteria. Could be classic interval scheduling: maximize sum of scores? Or maybe choose calls that are non-overlapping and also consider replacement tokens? The start and end are indices into tokens. range [start, end) perhaps. Replacement tokens list may replace the token span.\n\nLet's analyze the case: tokens = [\"t0\",\"t1\",...\"t9\"]. Calls:\na: start=1, end=3 (covers t1,t2? likely half-open?), replacement [\"<a>\"]\nb: start=2, end=5 (covers t2,t3,t4), replacement [\"<b>\"]\nc: start=7, end=9 (covers t7,t8), replacement [\"<c>\"]\n\nThese calls overlap: a and b overlap on position 2. So we cannot select both if we require non-overlapping. Select the one with higher score? b has score 0.9, a has 0.8, so select b. c is non-overlapping with b (b ends at 5, c starts at 7). So selected_call_ids = [\"b\",\"c\"]. Then tokens output: start with original tokens, replace selected call intervals with their replacement tokens. But what about tokens outside intervals? They remain. Also need to consider order: the replacement tokens may be multiple tokens? In these cases each replacement_tokens has one token. So for b: replace tokens t2,t3,t4 with \"<b>\". For c: replace t7,t8 with \"<c>\". The resulting token sequence: t0, t1, \"<b>\", t5, t6, \"<c>\", t9? Let's list indices: original: 0:t0,1:t1,2:t2,3:t3,4:t4,5:t5,6:t6,7:t7,8:t8,9:t9. Replace 2-4 with \"<b>\" => sequence becomes: t0,t1,\"<b>\",t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Then replace 7-8 (original indices) but after first replacement, indices shift. We need to apply replacements in order of start position, and adjust indices. Typically we would process from left to right, accounting for length differences. For overlapping selection we only select non-overlapping intervals, so order is fine. For selected intervals, we need to replace the span of original tokens with the replacement_tokens array. In case of a single replacement token, the span length is reduced. So resulting tokens: start with all tokens, then for each selected call in order of start, replace the segment with its replacement_tokens. For b (2-5? Note end=5, so indices 2,3,4? Assuming half-open: [start, end) covering indices 2,3,4), after replacing t2,t3,t4 with [\"<b>\"], sequence: t0,t1,\"<b>\",t5,t6,t7,t8,t9. Then for c (7-9 half-open covering 7,8), but after b, the index of original t7 is now at position 5? Let's compute: after b, tokens length becomes 8: indices:0:t0,1:t1,2:\"<b>\",3:t5,4:t6,5:t7,6:t8,7:t9. So original index 7 is now at position 5, original 8 at 6. So we need to apply replacements using original indices, but after modifications indices change. We can compute by applying in order of start and adjusting. Since calls are non-overlapping, we can apply sequentially from leftmost to rightmost, using original indices, and after each replacement adjust subsequent start/end by replacement_length - original_length. But it's easier to build the result by iterating through original tokens and skipping replaced segments while inserting replacement tokens.\n\nAlternatively, might be simpler: the tokens output is just the original tokens array with selected call replacements applied in order of start. Given the simplicity of the case, likely that's the intended output.\n\nBut we need to be sure about selection criteria. The case doesn't specify the objective. Perhaps it's a known problem: \"select a subset of calls with no overlaps to maximize the sum of scores\"? That is weighted interval scheduling. With only three calls, the optimal non-overlapping set with max score sum is {b,c} (0.9+0.7=1.6) vs {a,c} (0.8+0.7=1.5) vs {a} (0.8) vs {b} (0.9) vs {c} (0.7). So {b,c} is optimal. That seems plausible.\n\nWhat about calls that are adjacent? b ends at 5, c starts at 7, there is a gap at index 5 and 6 (t5,t6). That's fine.\n\nThus algorithm: given list of calls with start, end, score. Select non-overlapping subset maximizing sum of scores. Use weighted interval scheduling (DP). Then apply the selected calls to the tokens list to produce output tokens.\n\nBut we must also consider that replacement_tokens might have multiple tokens, which changes length. But the problem only cares about token output as final sequence.\n\nEdge cases: calls may have same start/end? Not here.\n\nSo implement solve(case):\n- Extract tokens and calls from case (case is the payload object, which contains \"tokens\" and \"calls\" lists).\n- Sort calls by end? For DP we need sorted by end.\n- Compute DP: dp[i] = max score using first i calls (sorted by end). Also track selection.\n- Reconstruct selected call_ids.\n- Then apply replacements: build result_tokens = [].\n- Iterate through original tokens index, but we have intervals. Use sorted selected calls by start.\n- Use pointer on tokens and for each selected call, append tokens from current index to start, then append replacement_tokens, then advance index to end.\n- After last call, append remaining tokens.\n- Return {\"selected_call_ids\": sorted list of selected call_ids? The order in output? Not specified, but likely could be in any order? The schema just says array of strings. We'll preserve order of selection (sorted by start). \n- For tokens output, we have result list.\n\nMake sure not to mutate case.\n\nWe need to output as Python source code string inside JSON with key \"implementation\". Escape properly.\n\nAlso note: the fixture shows payload includes \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore.\n\nThus I'll write solve function.\n\nBut must verify that the DP handles overlapping correctly. For weighted interval scheduling, we need to find for each call the last non-overlapping call before it. We'll sort by end.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Sort calls by end (ascending). If tie, maybe by start. But for reconstruction we need original call_ids.\n2. Compute prev list: for each call i, find the largest index j < i such that call[j].end <= call[i].start. (assuming intervals are half-open [start, end), so non-overlap is end <= start)\n3. DP: dp[i] = max(dp[i-1], dp[prev[i]] + call[i].score)\n4. Backtrack: start from i=len-1, if dp[i] == dp[i-1] then skip, else select call i and move to prev[i].\n5. After selection, get selected call_ids (original id from each selected call).\n6. Sort selected calls by start for applying replacements.\n\nThen apply replacements:\n    result_tokens = []\n    cursor = 0\n    for call in selected_sorted:\n        # append tokens from cursor to call['start']\n        result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:call['start']])\n        # append replacement tokens\n        result_tokens.extend(call['replacement_tokens'])\n        # update cursor\n        cursor = call['end']\n    # after last call, append remaining\n    result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:])\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nNote: The tokens in input are strings like \"t0\", etc. Replacement tokens are strings like \"<a>\".\n\nEdge: what if selected calls have overlapping start/end? Our selection ensures non-overlapping, so fine.\n\nOne nuance: The DP assumes we cannot select a call that overlaps with another. But what if two calls have same start/end? Not in this case.\n\nNow, the function signature: solve(case). case is the payload object with keys 'tokens' and 'calls'.\n\nWe'll need to import nothing extra (standard library only). Use Python built-ins.\n\nNow produce JSON output with \"implementation\" key.\n\nI must write the Python source as a string, escaping newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's craft the code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    tokens = case['tokens']\n    calls = case['calls']\n    # sort by end, then start\n    sorted_calls = sorted(calls, key=lambda c: (c['end'], c['start']))\n    n = len(sorted_calls)\n    # compute last non-overlapping index for each call\n    prev = [-1]*n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # binary search would be faster, but linear fine for small n\n        for j in range(i-1, -1, -1):\n            if sorted_calls[j]['end'] <= sorted_calls[i]['start']:\n                prev[i] = j\n                break\n    # dp\n    dp = [0]*n\n    for i in range(n):\n        score = sorted_calls[i]['score']\n        # choose i\n        choose = score + (dp[prev[i]] if prev[i] >=0 else 0)\n        # not choose\n        not_choose = dp[i-1] if i>0 else 0\n        dp[i] = max(choose, not_choose)\n    # backtrack to get selected indices\n    selected_indices = set()\n    i = n-1\n    while i >= 0:\n        if i==0:\n            if dp[0] > 0:\n                selected_indices.add(0)\n            break\n        score = sorted_calls[i]['score']\n        choose = score + (dp[prev[i]] if prev[i]>=0 else 0)\n        not_choose = dp[i-1]\n        if choose >= not_choose:  # prefer choose if equal? but need deterministic; greedy? choose if equal may be fine\n            selected_indices.add(i)\n            i = prev[i]\n        else:\n            i -= 1\n    # get call_ids\n    selected_call_ids = [sorted_calls[i]['call_id'] for i in selected_indices]\n    # sort selected calls by start\n    selected_calls = [sorted_calls[i] for i in selected_indices]\n    selected_calls.sort(key=lambda c: c['start'])\n    # apply\n    result_tokens = []\n    cursor = 0\n    for call in selected_calls:\n        start = call['start']\n        end = call['end']\n        result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:start])\n        result_tokens.extend(call['replacement_tokens'])\n        cursor = end\n    result_tokens.extend(tokens[cursor:])\n    return {\"selected_call_ids\": selected_call_ids, \"tokens\": result_tokens}\n\nCheck with given case: expected? Let's simulate: tokens 10, calls as above.\nSorted by end: a(end=3), b(end=5), c(end=9). prev: a: none (-1), b: a? a.end=3 <= b.start=2? No, 3>2, so no overlap? Actually overlapping, a.end=3 > b.start=2, so not non-overlapping. So prev for b is -1? Check condition j from i-1 down: for b(i=1), j=0: a.end=3 <= b.start=2? false, so no break, prev=-1. For c(i=2), j=1: b.end=5 <= c.start=7? true, so prev=1. Also j=0: a.end=3<=7 true but we take last? Actually we break on first found? We find j=1. prev=1.\nDP:\ndp[0]: choose=0.8+0=0.8, not_choose=0 => dp[0]=0.8\ndp[1]: choose=0.9 + (prev[1]=-1 =>0)=0.9, not_choose=dp[0]=0.8 => dp[1]=0.9\ndp[2]: choose=0.7 + dp[prev[2]=1]=0.7+0.9=1.6, not_choose=dp[1]=0.9 => dp[2]=1.6\nBacktrack i=2: choose=1.6 >= not_choose=0.9 => select i=2 (c), go to prev=1. i=1: now we need to decide for i=1. But note: after selecting i=2, we set i=prev[2]=1. At i=1, we need to decide whether to select it based on dp[1] vs? but we already have a path. Usually we don't evaluate again; we just go to prev[selected]. In backtrack: while i>=0: if i in selected_indices then we go to prev[i] and not consider not_choose. In our code above, we have a loop that checks choose vs not_choose at each i, but if we already selected i, we should move to prev i. In current code, for each i we recalc choose and not_choose and decide. That's fine. At i=1: score=0.9, choose=0.9+dp[prev[1]=-1]=0.9, not_choose=dp[0]=0.8 => choose >= not_choose, so select i=1 (b). Then i = prev[1]=-1, loop ends. So selected indices: {2,1} which are b and c. Then selected_call_ids = [\"b\",\"c\"]. Then selected_calls sorted by start: b(start=2), c(start=7). Apply: result_tokens: start cursor=0, b: append tokens[0:2] => [\"t0\",\"t1\"], then [\"<b>\"], cursor=2. Next c: append tokens[cursor:7] => tokens[2:7] since cursor=2? Wait tokens[2:7] gives [\"t2\",\"t3\",\"t4\",\"t5\",\"t6\"]? Actually original tokens indices 0-9. tokens[2:7] = [t2,t3,t4,t5,t6]. But b's interval was [2,5) so tokens[2:5] would be t2,t3,t4. But we haven't skipped the replaced part? In our code, we are using cursor=end of previous call, which is 5 after b. So after b, cursor=5. For c, we append tokens[5:7] => [t5,t6]. That is correct because tokens[2:5] were replaced by \"<b>\", so we should not include them. So tokens[5:7] is correct. Then append \"<c>\", cursor=9. After loop, append tokens[9:] => [t9]. So result_tokens = [\"t0\",\"t1\",\"<b>\",\"t5\",\"t6\",\"<c>\",\"t9\"]. That matches earlier expectation.\n\nThus algorithm seems correct.\n\nNow, need to ensure output matches schema exactly: must have keys \"selected_call_ids\" and \"tokens\". Both arrays of strings.\n\nPotential issues: The DP tie-breaking (choose >= not_choose) might cause different selection if scores equal; but in our case no ties.\n\nAlso note: The output tokens should be strings as per input.\n\nNow, produce final JSON with \"implementation\" key containing the Python source as a string.\n\nI'll write the code as a raw string, then escape for JSON.\n\nMake sure no extraneous commas, etc.\n\nI'll produce the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1374,"completion_tokens":4222,"total_tokens":5596,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1280},"comp